oxes. The key is to count the distinct partitions of the balls into up to 3 groups, regardless of the order of the groups.\n\n1. **All balls in one box**:\n   - This is straightforward: all 4 balls in one box. There is only 1 way to do this.\n   - **Count**: 1 way\n\n2. **3 balls in one box, 1 ball in another**:\n   - We choose 3 out of the 4 balls to be in one box, and the remaining 1 ball in another box.\n   - The number of ways to choose 3 balls out of 4 is given by the binomial coefficient:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{4}{3} = 4\n     \\]\n   - **Count**: 4 ways\n\n3. **2 balls in one box, 2 balls in another**:\n   - We choose 2 out of the 4 balls to be in one box, and the remaining 2 balls in another box.\n   - The number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4 is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{4}{2} = 6\n     \\]\n   - However, since the boxes are indistinguishable, swapping the two boxes does not create a new arrangement. Therefore, we divide by 2:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n     \\]\n   - **Count**: 3 ways\n\n4. **2 balls in one box, 1 ball in another, 1 ball in another**:\n   - We choose 2 out of the 4 balls to be in one box. The remaining 2 balls can each go into separate boxes.\n   - The number of ways to choose 2 balls out of 4 is:\n     \\[\n     \\binom{4}{2} = 6\n     \\]\n   - The remaining 2 balls will naturally go into the remaining 2 boxes, and since the boxes are indistinguishable, this counts as a single distribution.\n   - **Count**: 6 ways\n\nSumming up all the possible ways:\n\\[\n1 + 4 + 3 + 6 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to put 4 distinguishable balls into 3 indistinguishable boxes is \\(\\boxed{14}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{14}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
